{
  "id": "conjunctions",
  "level": "A2",
  "title": "Konjunktionen und Als-Wenn-Wann-Ob",
  "group": "Word Order",
  "sourceFile": "konjunktionen.html",
  "overviewSource": "konjunktionenexpl.html",
  "overview": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": "A subordinate clause cannot normally stand alone. It is separated by a comma, and its conjugated verb goes to the end."
      },
      {
        "type": "table",
        "content": "",
        "rows": [
          [
            "Conjunction",
            "Use",
            "Example idea"
          ],
          [
            "weil",
            "reason",
            "because"
          ],
          [
            "dass",
            "reported fact/opinion",
            "that"
          ],
          [
            "wenn",
            "condition/repeated or present-future time",
            "if/when"
          ],
          [
            "als",
            "one event in the past",
            "when"
          ],
          [
            "ob",
            "indirect yes/no question",
            "whether"
          ],
          [
            "obwohl",
            "contrast",
            "although"
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": "When the subordinate clause comes first, the whole clause counts as position 1, so the main-clause verb follows immediately: Weil ich müde bin, gehe ich nach Hause."
      },
      {
        "type": "example",
        "content": "",
        "de": "Er fragt, ob du morgen kommst.",
        "note": "ob introduces an indirect yes/no question"
      }
    ]
  },
